    {
      "id": "r20261110-a02",
      "title": "The Rise of the Four-Day Week",
      "level": "Menengah",
      "min": 3,
      "body": [
        "Imagine working from Monday to Thursday and having a three-day weekend every week. This is not a dream. Companies in several countries have tried a four-day working week, and many never went back.",
        "In most trials, workers keep their full salary but work one day less. The results surprised many managers: productivity stayed the same or even rose. Workers were less tired, took fewer sick days, and stayed at their jobs longer.",
        "The four-day week does not fit every job, of course. Hospitals and restaurants still need people every day. But for office workers, the idea is spreading fast. \"I use my Fridays for my family,\" says one worker. \"I am happier, and strangely, I finish more work.\""
      ],
      "gloss": {
        "trials": "uji coba",
        "salary": "gaji",
        "productivity": "produktivitas",
        "sick days": "hari sakit",
        "fit": "cocok",
        "spreading": "menyebar",
        "strangely": "anehnya"
      },
      "qs": [
